WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
The boundary around serialized data changes the risk.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:13.080
A file parser must handle missing files, truncated files, stale versions, partial writes, path assumptions, and metadata confusion.

3
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:23.740
File names, extensions, directory locations, and timestamps can provide context, but they are not a substitute for validating the serialized content itself.

4
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.260
A network parser has different concerns.

5
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:37.320
It must handle framing, partial I/O, timeouts, retransmission, oversized messages, peers that disconnect, and messages that arrive out of order for the application protocol.

6
00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:43.660
The parser should know when a complete message has arrived before it attempts to interpret fields or make state changes.

7
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.860
Storage boundaries also matter.

8
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:56.680
Database records, cache entries, queue messages, and telemetry blobs may be old, partially migrated, or created by a previous version of the system.

9
00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.340
A cache hit should not automatically mean the data is current and safe.

10
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.840
Compatibility rules and validation still apply when data comes from infrastructure the application usually trusts.

11
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:14.200
Logs and telemetry may contain sensitive serialized fields that require redaction or minimization.

12
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:25.180
Boundary assumptions should be documented: what data is covered by validation, what is covered by integrity checks, what is encrypted, and which compatibility rules apply.

13
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.160
Clear boundaries make audits and incident response much easier.

14
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.920
The same serialized format may appear in more than one boundary over time.

15
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:41.260
A file format may later be sent over a network, or a cache entry may become a queue message.

16
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.080
Review assumptions whenever the boundary changes.
